{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdYqtexvDVKuAkRiev5e","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Google's Suncatcher prototype carries four TPUs but can run only one at a time, and only for about 20 minutes, because the satellite generates up to 1 kW of electricity. The spacecraft is now online and talking to the ground after launching this month on a Falcon 9 with more than 100 other spacecraft. Its first test prompt asked the preloaded Gemma 4 model for a haiku about being in space. The goal is networks of close-flying satellites performing large inference workloads in orbit, and the power limit shows how far the prototype is from that. It also complicates space-to-ground communications, since the compute is available only in short windows.\n\nThe next step is already sketched. If the TPUs prove capable, Google plans to launch two more satellites in 2027 with higher power generation and inter-satellite optical links. The current campaign decides whether that happens by testing the chips under thermal stress and in the space radiation environment. Radiation can cause bit flips that corrupt data without triggering typical monitoring tools. Google's ground testing, including particle accelerator tests, suggested the effect would be minimal for inference and more of a problem for training, and orbit is where that finding gets checked against real conditions.\n\nEven if the chips hold up, the cost of reaching orbit remains. Both Google and SpaceX need Starship, which is still in development, to become fully and rapidly reusable for their orbital data centers to make economic sense. Alphabet holds a significant investment stake in SpaceX, so the two visions are closely linked. In a peer-reviewed analysis published this month, Suncatcher researchers estimated that SpaceX would need to fly Starship 1,800 times to achieve the same learning-driven cost reductions it saw with earlier vehicles. Suncatcher is aimed at infrastructure that will be economical in the 2030s, which means the prototype's results on radiation will matter only if launch costs fall far enough."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1931,"cache_read_input_tokens":2715,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1931},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5ea27f4b-c52f-4580-a7f5-b3a6b7a08186","uuid":"0598c1c9-5c74-4c27-a9b4-cc9cd1363235","timestamp":"2026-11-02T05:29:01.265Z","request_id":"req_011CgdYqt9wsAtAqQeqkGVjL"}
